From 02393e6ca3919b4ca765afef9ab4d97ad11e75a8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 12:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] xen: domctl: zero the createdomain hypercall argument

xen_domctl_createdomain() is the only function in drivers/xen/dom0/domctl.c
that declares its xen_domctl_t without an initialiser; the other nineteen use
a designated initialiser, which zeroes the members they do not name. It is
also the one where that matters.

struct xen_domctl_createdomain ends in struct xen_arch_domainconfig, and
Zephyr vendors its own copy of that struct. On arm64 the vendored version is
12 bytes with CONFIG_XEN_DOMCTL_XENTROOPS_ARCH_{SCI_TYPE,VGSX_OSID}=n, while
Xen 4.21's is 16: Xen carries a trailing uint8_t arm_sci_type (added by its
generic SCI subsystem) that this tree has no field for. `domctl.u.createdomain
= *config` copies the shorter struct, so the byte Xen reads as arm_sci_type
comes from uninitialised stack.

It is harmless in the configuration this series builds -- Xen's
sci_domain_sanitise_config() returns 0 as soon as no SCI mediator is
registered, and none is unless CONFIG_SCMI_SMC is selected -- but it becomes a
sporadic domain-creation failure the moment an SCI mediator is present, since
sanitising then rejects any arm_sci_type other than the two defined values.
Zeroing the argument makes the byte read as XEN_DOMCTL_CONFIG_ARM_SCI_NONE,
which is what this Dom0 means.

While here, the three assignments that follow ended in commas rather than
semicolons, which made the last of them a comma expression with the do_domctl()
call. That parses and behaves correctly; it is still a typo.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-4-8
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
diff --git a/drivers/xen/dom0/domctl.c b/drivers/xen/dom0/domctl.c
index e642ac04a4..2355996a8a 100644
--- a/drivers/xen/dom0/domctl.c
+++ b/drivers/xen/dom0/domctl.c
@@ -277,15 +277,25 @@ int xen_domctl_max_vcpus(int domid, int max_vcpus)
 int xen_domctl_createdomain(int *domid, struct xen_domctl_createdomain *config)
 {
 	int ret;
-	xen_domctl_t domctl;
+	/*
+	 * Zero the whole hypercall argument. This is the only xen_domctl_t in
+	 * this file without an initialiser, and it is the one that matters:
+	 * struct xen_domctl_createdomain ends in xen_arch_domainconfig, and the
+	 * copy below writes only as many bytes as *this* tree's version of that
+	 * struct has. Xen reads its own, which is longer whenever it carries a
+	 * field we do not vendor -- on Xen 4.21/arm64 that is the trailing
+	 * arm_sci_type byte -- so without this the hypervisor would read
+	 * uninitialised stack for it.
+	 */
+	xen_domctl_t domctl = { 0 };
 
 	if (!domid || !config) {
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
-	domctl.cmd = XEN_DOMCTL_createdomain,
-	domctl.domain = *domid,
-	domctl.u.createdomain = *config,
+	domctl.cmd = XEN_DOMCTL_createdomain;
+	domctl.domain = *domid;
+	domctl.u.createdomain = *config;
 
 	ret = do_domctl(&domctl);
 	*domid = domctl.domain;
